<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      Permissions the ACTIONS this app allows a model to request need — see `ActionAllowlist`, which
      declares each one beside the intent that needs it.

      This was missing, and it is why tool calling appeared broken on device. `SET_ALARM` and
      `SET_TIMER` both require `com.android.alarm.permission.SET_ALARM`. The model produced a correct
      call, the validator accepted it, the card offered Run — and `startActivity` threw
      SecurityException, which reads as "the feature does not work" rather than as "a line is missing
      here". Nothing could have caught it off-device: the validator and the binder are both correct,
      and a permission is only enforced when an intent is actually started.

      This is an install-time (`normal`) permission, so declaring it IS granting it — there is no
      runtime dialog for it, and a UI that offers to ask for it would be describing something that
      cannot happen.

      Declared in the APP rather than the library on purpose: which actions to permit is the
      application's decision, and an SDK that silently added alarm permissions to every consumer would
      be claiming a capability on their behalf.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="com.android.alarm.permission.SET_ALARM" />

    <application
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:largeHeap="true"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.MobileTransformersApp"
        tools:targetApi="31">
        <!--
          `.TrainingScreen` and `.InferenceScreen` were declared here long after the classes behind
          them were deleted in the facade rewrite. A manifest naming activities that do not exist is
          a crash waiting for whoever first tries to start one, and it misdescribes the app: this is
          a single-activity Compose app, and its screens are composables, not activities.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />

                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>

</manifest>